it was a gruesome scene inside the  littoral penitentiary in ecuador's  largest city guayaquil  police confirmed they'd found more than  100 bodies of inmates six of them  beheaded  at least 50 were injured two of them  police officers  while desperate relatives surrounded the  prison outside president guillermo lasso  met with his security council and  declared a state of emergency in the  country's entire penitentiary system  we want justice mr president at least  show your face for all the mothers who  suffer here for our children  we want information because we don't  know anything about our families or our  children because i have a son here i  don't know anything about my son  inside the prison there were machine  guns explosives knives a full arsenal as  two rival drug gangs the los lobos and  los torneros fought it out  the riots began tuesday but they weren't  the first at least 220 people have died  behind bars this year in two separate  prison riots in ecuador  in the first at least 80 men died in  rioting in three different prisons two  months ago another riot prompted  president died molasso to also declare a  state of emergency in the prison system  but the fragile truce didn't last long  ecuador's prisons are understaffed  overcrowded and full of rival gangs  armed to the teeth  the former director of military  intelligence says that the gangs worked  for mexico's two top rival drug cartels  and that this latest massacre  illustrates just how far organized crime  has penetrated the prison structure  president lasso promises to carry out a  full inquiry and respect the human  rights of all those involved  but diffusing the country's prison  powder keg will clearly require much  much more  lucia newman al jazeera
 

